## Context

The parent spec locks in a hard rule: AIDE doctrine lives in docs/,
and the install service is a renderer. That rule has to be enforced
somewhere, and the cleanest place to enforce it is at the single
chokepoint where the install service subtree touches the canonical
docs. This helper is that chokepoint. Everything above it in the call
graph either composes or installs; nothing else reads.

Centralizing the read has a second benefit: if docs/ ever moves, its
layout changes, or a new canonical doc is added, the edit radius is
one file. A design that scatters `readFile("docs/...")` calls across
the install-service helpers would pay the cost of that centrality
every time anything changed about how the canonical source is
stored.

## Strategy

**Verbatim reads, no transformation.** The helper's only job is to
return the bytes on disk. It does not parse frontmatter, extract
sections, normalize whitespace, or rewrite links. Consumers that
need a transformation own it end-to-end, because the reasoning for
each transform belongs next to the code that uses the result.
Keeping this helper transformation-free means there is exactly one
interpretation of "canonical content" — the file on disk — and no
ambiguity about where a given rewrite decision was made.

**Sole reader of docs/ by convention and by code review.** The
other install-service helpers import from initContent and never from
the filesystem. This is enforced by convention in the current pass
and by a regression test in the follow-up implementation pass. Any
future PR that adds a second reader of docs/ under the install
service subtree is a design regression and should be rejected in
review.

**Fail loudly on missing source.** Unreadable or missing canonical
docs raise an error naming the missing file. There is no fallback
to an embedded default, because any fallback would silently ship
stale content and defeat the entire point of the chokepoint. An
init that aborts with "docs/automated-qa.md not found" is a visible
bug; an init that silently serves a six-month-old copy of the same
file is an invisible one.

**Lookup by canonical name, not by path.** Consumers ask for the
methodology doc, the template doc, the QA doc by name; this helper
owns the mapping from name to file. If docs/ is reorganized, the
mapping changes in this file and nowhere else. Exposing file paths
across the interface would leak the folder layout into every
consumer and lose the centrality benefit.

**Enumeration of named subsets lives here too.** Knowing which
canonical docs exist is one kind of source-of-truth decision;
knowing which of those docs make up "the methodology" is the same
kind of decision, and it lives at the same chokepoint. Consumers
that operate on the methodology subset ask this helper to
enumerate it and iterate whatever list comes back, so that adding
or removing a methodology doc is one edit in the same registry
that already owns the name-to-path mapping. Any downstream list
hardcoded to the methodology set would silently drift on the next
addition — and the farther from the registry the hardcoded list
lives, the longer the drift stays invisible.

## Good examples

writeMethodology asks initContent for the canonical spec body, the
template body, and the progressive-disclosure body. initContent
reads three files from docs/, returns them verbatim as strings, and
writeMethodology composes the methodology block from the bytes it
receives. A later edit to any of those three docs propagates to
every host project's next init with zero changes to writeMethodology
and zero changes to initContent.

scaffoldCommands asks initContent for the pipeline command
templates by name. initContent reads the template files from
docs/commands/aide/ and returns them as a map from canonical name
to file content. scaffoldCommands writes each one to an `aide/`
subfolder of the host's command directory without mutating the
returned strings.

A maintainer moves the canonical docs from docs/ to docs/canonical/.
They update initContent's internal path resolution in one place.
No other init helper is touched. The next init run on a host project
reads the new location, and no host-project-visible behavior
changes.

## Bad examples

initContent reads `docs/aide-spec.md`, strips the frontmatter,
removes the top-level H1, and returns the trimmed body. A later
consumer needs the frontmatter for a different purpose and cannot
get it, because the reader helped too eagerly. The transformation
should have happened in the consumer, not the reader.

writeMethodology bypasses initContent and opens `docs/aide-spec.md`
directly "because it's simpler". initContent is no longer the sole
reader; a later edit to docs/ (or a rename) breaks one helper and
not the other; the chokepoint invariant is gone and no single
review will catch the drift.

initContent catches a read failure on `docs/progressive-disclosure.md`
and returns an empty string. writeMethodology happily composes a
methodology block with a missing Progressive Disclosure section.
The host project installs a partial methodology, the agent reasons
about the AIDE tiers from the missing material, and no one knows a
section is gone because the init summary reported success.

initContent is "optimized" by embedding the canonical doc bodies as
string constants at the top of the file, read once at module load.
The file's header comment says "this mirrors docs/ for performance;
edits must be made in both places." The invariant has been inverted
into a maintenance checklist — and the next maintainer who edits
docs/ without remembering the checklist ships two sources of truth.
